WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.008 --> 00:00:01.300
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Hi, Martin.

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.700
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Valentin.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.350
I heard you wrote
a web app and would

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.420
like to run it in the cloud?

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.730
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Yes,
I wrote a simple web app.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.130
Also, I love
serverless, and I need

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.530
to deploy my app to the cloud.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.660
Today, we'll explore
three different ways

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.375
I can make that happen.

00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:20.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.620
You know I'm a big
fan of QR codes.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.960
They're one of the
most underrated pieces

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.117
of brilliant technology
of the last decades.

00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:39.430
MARTIN OMANDER: [LAUGHS]
Well, they're beautiful.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.500
So what do you want to
do with these things?

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.167
VALENTIN DELEPLACE:
Today, I'll show you

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:45.670
a nice cloud app called
Hot Maze to transfer

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.480
a file from my computer
to my mobile phone.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.030
For this, I could
plug a USB cable,

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:57.430
or maybe send myself an email,
or use Dropbox or Google Drive,

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.930
but I prefer QR codes.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.540
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
that sounds neat.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.590
Tell me more.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.900
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: I'll
make a very quick demo.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.220
Here, I have a picture of
a gopher on my computer.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:14.740
I drag and drop it here, and
bam, I'm generating a QR code.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:18.430
Then I scan the QR
code, and then I

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.440
have the gopher on my phone.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:21.670
That's it.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.140
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, cool.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.830
Does it also work with
video files or text files?

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.050
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Yes, you
can send any file you want.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:30.890
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.860
So this is a cloud service?

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:33.860
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Yes.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.220
The most simple way to achieve
a transfer between the two

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.070
devices who don't know
each other very well

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.070
is to have the computer
upload to the cloud

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.980
and the mobile download
from the cloud.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:46.880
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.340
So where do you
host the web page,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.503
and where do you
upload the files?

00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:53.420
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: I
have a strong preference

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.290
for serverless, because I
don't have to manage instances

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.690
myself, because of cost--

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.090
I don't pay when
it's not in use--

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.980
and because of automatic
scaling in case

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.120
I have thousands of
friends using the service

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.060
at the same time to
upload their own gophers.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.230
In Google Cloud, I'm spoiled for
choices of serverless computing

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.820
hosting options.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.100
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, so
which one did you choose?

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.090
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: All of them.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.210
There are several valid
combinations to choose from.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.780
Let's explore three
possible options.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.940
First, App Engine Standard.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.560
I've been loving this
one even before the word

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.450
serverless existed.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:33.950
See?

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.080
App Engine can both host my
HTML and static resources

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.380
and process what happens
in the cloud backend

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.430
when I drop a file,
executing my source code.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.040
First, I created a
regular Go web server

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.890
that I tested locally
on my workstation.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.400
Then I could deploy it without
modifying the source code.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.480
This is the command line I
used to deploy to App Engine.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.140
I chose Go out of six languages
available in App Engine

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.450
Standard.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.790
On file drop, we
asked the web app

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.490
to generate two secure URLs--

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.850
one to let the browser
upload to Cloud Storage,

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:17.480
and one to let the phone
download from Cloud Storage.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:18.530
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.350
But so far, the server is
only connected to the web

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.210
page in the computer browser.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.270
How can you push
anything to the phone?

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.310
You don't even know
its IP address.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.680
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: That's
the beauty of the QR code.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.790
The web page encodes the secure
download URL inside the QR

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:38.330
code, and this is all the info
that is needed for the phone

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:41.300
to retrieve the file
when scanning the QR code

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.760
and extracting the URL.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.270
MARTIN OMANDER: Amazing.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.820
So to summarize,
you did all this

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.570
using App Engine
and Cloud Storage?

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:50.570
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Yes.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.010
That was option number one.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.000
Option number two is
Google Cloud Functions.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.480
You see, functions are great
to quickly implement something

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.850
to do in response to an event.

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.610
For example, our
events are someone

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.910
just drag and dropped a
file, and someone just

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.100
scanned a URL QR code.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.980
We also need to host
the web page itself.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.190
And for this, I recommend
Firebase Hosting.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:20.269
I have a command to deploy each
function and another command

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:23.720
to deploy the website's
static assets.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.480
MARTIN OMANDER: So Firebase
Hosting for the frontend

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.790
and Cloud Functions
for the backend logic,

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.880
and Cloud Storage for
the temporary file?

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.420
VALENTIN DELEPLACE:
That's right.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.070
Functions are a modern approach
for developing services that

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:41.900
are stateless, loosely coupled,
that process incoming events

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.350
and communicate with each
other by message passing.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.510
They're a good fit for
fine-grained microservices.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:49.550
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.120
You said you had a third
serverless alternative?

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.120
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Yes.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.240
We can deploy the whole
thing, frontend and backend,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.160
to Cloud Run.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.670
For my app, it's very similar
to deploying to App Engine,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.470
but with explicit containers.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.640
So if you already have a
container-driven workflow

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.840
and use a container registry,
then deploying to Cloud Run

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:13.410
will be seamless.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.540
You can also install almost
any binary dependency

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.770
you need in a container, which
is mindblowing, in my opinion.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.380
If, like me, you don't
know that much about Docker

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.310
files and container
technologies,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.400
though, you may like the
ready-to-use buildpacks

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.620
for deployment.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:31.850
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.407
Valentin, what's
your recommendation?

00:05:34.407 --> 00:05:36.740
VALENTIN DELEPLACE: Well, the
three serverless solutions

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.520
work really well, because
the app design is stateless

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.700
and all these platforms, except
Go, has a backend language.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.970
They all achieve
high scalability

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:49.160
and cost efficiency.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.800
If you want to know more
about the small differences

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.605
in the developer experience
and in the infrastructure

00:05:54.605 --> 00:05:57.395
more than trade-offs,
please check the articles

00:05:57.395 --> 00:05:59.780
linked in the description.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.240
MARTIN OMANDER: So I have
hundreds of photos and videos

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.130
of cats on my hard drive.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.890
Could I use your app to
copy them to my phone?

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.660
VALENTIN DELEPLACE:
Photos of cats?

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:09.620
Really?

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.920
Yes, I guess you could
use my app for that.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.190
Thank you for watching.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.890
You can also find
the source code

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.260
for this app in the
description below.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.710
Feel free to add any questions
you might have in the comments.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

